				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">The icy hills of the Great Divide were dark under the new moon, a chill wind blowing across the land, carrying stinging pieces of ice and snow. Crouched in a low stand of stunted trees, a small group huddled together for warmth, silent and fearful. Far beyond the next rise the mournful howl of a wolf was carried to their ears, and they shivered and huddled closer into their capes. Another howl answered the first, and suddenly there were howls all around them, from the unseen throats of many wolves hunting the night, and it seemed they were surrounded. The sound echoed off the frozen rocks and shook snow from the boughs of the trees above the cowering group. One young human cleric closed her eyes and let a single tear leak from them, which froze halfway down her cheek. 
<br />

<br />
The leader of the group, a stunted gnome wizard clothed in thick robes of sky blue, glanced her way with a frown. Imagine the woman's weakness! He snorted to himself and cast his sunken eyes about, peering into the darkness for a glimpse of the howling creatures. He knew it was a trick of the hills to make them sound more than they were, and he snickered at their lack of subtlety. Surely they would be unable to resist the scent of their warm blood and soon would come slinking towards the little valley to be picked off easily. So he hoped. Having once run with the pack himself, he thought he knew their limits.
<br />

<br />
Over the ridge that hid the valley and its cowering occupants, a tall elf man lay in the snow, low to the ground to avoid being seen, and peered into the night. His eyes were sharp, as were the eyes of all elves, and he could easily make out the forms of those huddled in the grove. He beckoned to his companies and they crept foward.
<br />

<br />
"There they are." He whispered, pointing at the trees. "Hidden, so they think. They would have ported in just over there." He nodded towards a crumbling stone pad, the once beautiful staging point where Wizards and Druids of Norrath could transport, now fell into disuse with the discovery of more exciting lands. 
<br />

<br />
A thick barbarian warrior, unscathed by the cold, nodded. "Aye, that's how they got around us, though we guarded the only pass through from the Planes." 
<br />

<br />
The elf smiled, a smile that spoke of blood to be spilled and pleasure in the coming kill. "Little good it'll do them, crafty bastards. They'll freeze in there unless they port out or try to gain the pass. We stay."
<br />

<br />
His companions nodded. They were about a dozen all told, all races and walks of life, but they were all Wolves. And nothing was more fearsome than the Wolfpack on the Hunt. And tonight, their prey was no monster, no dragon, but a more sinister enemy, the most painful and wounding enemy of all, a former ally and friend. The GuildHouse known as "No Tears" and their tag-a-long apprentices, the Initiates. Some of those hidden on the ridge felt misgivings to attack old friends, misgivings that were mixed with anger. It was a volatile emotion, fueling the fires of war. A few said quick prayers to their gods, and others remembered past transgressions, especially the night in the Temple of Ssrasheza when two wolves were severely wounded at the hands of their "allies". The thought hardened their resolve. Weapons were gripped more firmly. Spells were hastily recited and committed to memory. 
<br />

<br />
The battle that night was long. There were losses on both sides. Continually the Wolves drove the Tears back to their hiding places, to wait patiently for a renewed attack. They came in waves, but the defense held strong. By night's end, as both sides left the field to go back to their Houses for a night's rest, the battlefield was littered with corpses, but there was a song in the hearts of the Wolves. They had held strong and worked as a pack. They felt the battle had been theirs.
<br />

<br />
And in the long weeks to follow, as battle was engaged continually all over Norrath, the resolve did not falter. Stronger they pressed the attack, seeming to be possessed of some God's favor as they not only defeated their enemies time and time again, but continued their day-to-day activities of adventuring and hunting down creatures of fame for their treasure. On both sides of the war were mutters of wondering its purpose, but the core held strong and firm. There would be no peace until Wolf blood and betrayal was fully revenged.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=956867" target="_blank" class="postlink">~Sriani HeartofWolf~</a>
<br />
66 Lady of War
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold">Wolves of Zek Recruit Council</span></div></span><span class="gensmall"></span></td>
