			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">The Guildhouse of the Covenant of the Wolf was less a guild house than a large, sprawling family home. It was filled with light and the warmth from fireplaces and torches lining the hallways. Exquisetly worked Dwarven mosaics decorated the walls, depicted past Guild Leaders and courageous deeds and victories. Normally it was a place of laughter and companionship, where the guildmembers could relax after a day's work with a good meal, song, stories and games. However now it was a fortress in a time of war.
<br />

<br />
Battle had been pitched all over Norrath for weeks now. Daily the bloodied and battered members rode into the gates, or walked, or limped, to fix armor, eat a meal, or simply sleep the exhausted sleep of the soldier. Scouts ran in and out like ants, carrying messages from all over the land on the enemy's movements and numbers. At the head of this buzzing army was a young elf druid, who sat in a chair at the head table, surrounded by maps, papers, and the constantly shifting members of her Council. As some left for the battle front, and others returned from it, she listened patiently to all news and advice. Word of members leaving the guild's roster in protest of the war were like arrows to her heart, but she let nothing show on her face. Her face was like stone, and her eyes were hard cold diamonds. Anyone who did not know her would think her unfeeling.
<br />

<br />
From the kitchens, one of her new warriors entered the hall carrying a cup of steaming tea. Raveena smiled to herself. She kept thinking "new" even though the woman clad all in shining black armor had been a member for quite some months now, and taken to the pack like the eldest wolf. She looked up now from her endless stack of papers (queries from other guilds as to the war's origins and status, demands from the enemy, complaints from various members, reports on battles, letters of encouragement and more) and into the woman's face. A long shock of red hair framed a pale face, hardened from battle but with a twinkle of laughter in her eyes, as if she was always on the verge of telling a very good joke.
<br />

<br />
"Raveena, Guild Mistress, you really must drink some tea." Sriani said without preamble, setting the cup down right on top of a stack of papers. Raveena made as if to move the cup immedietly, but Sriani gently laid a hand on hers. "Leave the papers a moment and drink. If you don't relax for a minute those lines on your face will become permanent."
<br />

<br />
Raveena sighed in defeat, but with a smile tugging her lips, and raised the cup to sip. It was fragrant and relaxing, as promised, some herb she didn't know the name of. For once, amazingly, the Council Hall was empty except for herself and the warrior woman, and a blessed silence surrounded her. She closed her eyes and held the cup close to her face, absorbing its calming aroma and trying to just stop thinking for a minute. 
<br />

<br />
She heard a shuffle of papers and opened one eye. Sriani had seized a stack of them that was set off to one side. Normally Raveena would reprimand such impertinance, but for now she felt too calm to raise her voice, and simply watched the other woman read. At last Sriani looked up, her face stony.
<br />

<br />
"These are them." She asked, her voice hard. Raveena didn't need to ask what she meant. She nodded slowly. "Trash. Garbage. How dare anyone write this!" Sriani cried in sudden anger, throwing the papers to the floor. They scattered, some caught the draft from the door and were whisked across the room. "You don't beleive it do you?"
<br />

<br />
Raveena met the woman's eyes for a moment and then looked away, unable to bear the frank challenge in them. How she wished she could feel such pure emotion, untainted by her million duties. How she wished that just for once she could feel for herself, and not worry about 200 people's needs and futures, nevermind the politics with other guilds. 
<br />

<br />
"Well do you?" Sriani demanded.
<br />

<br />
"Of course I don't." Raveena spoke softly, sipping from the cup again. She closed her eyes once more and laid her head back on the chair. "But still...it hurts. To read those things about yourself. To know that someone thinks them. It makes one feel...that all your accomplishments...are nothing. Meaningless. Unappreciated."
<br />

<br />
Her voice caught on this last word and she knew she was perilously close to tears. Sriani sat down close to her and took her hand.
<br />

<br />
"Raveena, I know. I led my guild, though I led it into the ground some say, I led it to the best of my ability, and there's always someone somewhere who thinks they can do it better. Who, for whatever reason, grudges you and some imagined slight. You can't let it get under your skin, because if you do, you'll go mad with it." She squeezed the elf's hand and smiled into her teary eyes. "You know none of us would see you fall, and not a word of those horrid letters are true. We wage this war for you, for your sanity and your honor. Because we're family, and that's what families do."
<br />

<br />
Raveena nodded, and squeezed back. For a moment the two women were silent, each with her own thoughts. Finally, Raveena nodded.
<br />

<br />
"You're right. Of course you're right. It's silly to let it get to me." She smiled shakily and set down the cup. "Thank you for the tea."
<br />

<br />
Sriani nodded, standing to gather up the papers she had let fall. She folded them up neatly and tucked them under a stone on the table. Then she bowed low to her leader and walked out of the hall. Raveena stared after her for a moment, and then turned back to her papers. She threw back her shoulders and a new determined look crossed her face. She'd be damned if she let those nasty letters get to her. And she'd be damned if she let the writers go unpunished. 
<br />

<br />
"For family honor." She whispered to herself. "For Wolf honor."
<br />

<br />
And from the shadows of the doorway where he'd stood silently for some time, a green-armored elf smiled too.<div style="vertical-align: bottom"><br /><br /><br />_________________<br /><a href="http://www.magelo.com/eq_view_profile.html?num=956867" target="_blank" class="postlink">~Sriani HeartofWolf~</a>
<br />
